course: dictionary
lesson: 10795
title: Vanish: gone in a flash
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To vanish means to disappear suddenly, in a way you cannot explain. In «A magician in Dublin made a girl's watch vanish from a box», the watch was there one moment and gone the next, so vanish must mean disappear.
explain: The word has a second meaning too. Some animals and old ways of life have vanished, which means they have stopped existing for ever. Look at the words around vanish to tell which meaning is in use: a magic trick, a countdown or a sudden disappearance points to the first, while words like for ever point to the second.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence. If "disappear" fits and the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right meaning. Vanish is a verb, so it changes with time: vanish, vanishes, vanished, vanishing.
fact: To vanish means to disappear suddenly or in a way you cannot explain.
fact: To vanish can also mean to stop existing, like an animal or a way of life that is gone for ever.
fact: Forms of vanish: vanish, vanishes, vanished, vanishing.
fact: Phrases: «vanish into thin air», «vanishing act», «vanish without a trace».
fact: «Real bargains rarely vanish in minutes», so a rush like «Hurry, only today!» is often a trick to stop you thinking clearly.
exercise: «A magician in Dublin made a girl's watch vanish from a box.» What does vanish mean here? | Break into small pieces | *Disappear in a way you cannot explain | Ring loudly
exercise: A science book says a kind of frog has vanished. What does this mean? | It has gone to sleep for a while | *It has stopped existing for ever | It has been painted a new colour
exercise: Which word means the opposite of vanish? | shout | run | *appear | sleep
test: «Real chameleons change colour mostly to show their mood and control their temperature, not to hide.» Does this mean chameleons vanish to hide? | Yes, they always vanish to hide from danger | Yes, they vanish into thin air | *No, the sentence says they change colour for mood and temperature, not to hide
test: The magician's watch came straight back with a bow. Why is this called a vanishing act, not a real loss? | The girl hid the watch | The watch was lost for ever | The watch was broken | *The watch came straight back, so it was a trick
exam: Which phrase means the watch was there, then gone, with no sign left at all? | Give a bow | *Vanish without a trace | Wake up early | Keep the secret
